      "nets": ["+5V", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet, LED1 is connected from +5V in series with C72 (2u2) to GND. There is no series current-limiting resistor in this path.",
      "expected": "A steady power indicator should use a series current-limiting resistor. A capacitor in series blocks steady-state current and does not provide reliable LED current limiting.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "LED1; C72 2u2; +5V; GND (Power sheet, page 1/6)"
        },
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "LED2 is shown with R8 240 in its indicator path (Power sheet, page 1/6)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "5 V rail: A MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V. (Power Section, §4)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "LED1 will at most produce a transient flash as C72 charges, rather than indicate that the 5 V rail is present; the initial current pulse may also stress the LED.",
      "confidence": 0.87
    }
  ],
  "summary": "An anomaly is present in the 5 V indicator circuit: LED1 is in series with a capacitor instead of a current-limiting resistor, so it will not provide a steady power indication."
}

